# Open Source Leg — Ankle-Only Mode

This branch adapts the [Open Source Leg (OSL)](https://github.com/neurobionics/opensourceleg) codebase for a **modular ankle-only configuration**, where the knee joint and actuator are physically removed and a single Raspberry Pi controls only the ankle Dephy actuator.

## What this is

This is **not** a fork or rewrite of the OSL library. The core `opensourceleg/` package (`actuators/`, `robots/`, `sensors/`, `control/`) is used exactly as UMich's Neurobionics Lab published it — nothing in that package has been modified. This branch only adds new, ankle-only scripts on top of the existing library.

If you're running the standard two-joint leg, none of this affects you — use the library and examples as normal.

## Why this branch exists

The library's `OpenSourceLeg` class assigns joint roles (`"knee"`, `"ankle"`) purely by **dictionary key**, not by any hardware detection:

```python
osl = OpenSourceLeg(
    actuators={
        "knee": DephyActuator(tag="knee", port="/dev/ttyACM0", ...),
        "ankle": DephyActuator(tag="ankle", port="/dev/ttyACM1", ...),
    },
)
```

The two-joint example scripts hardcode `"knee"` to `/dev/ttyACM0`. On Linux, `/dev/ttyACM0` vs `/dev/ttyACM1` is assigned by whichever USB serial device the kernel enumerates first — it is **not** tied to which joint is physically knee or ankle. If you run a two-joint script like this on a Pi with only the ankle actuator connected, the ankle actuator will land on `/dev/ttyACM0` and get mislabeled `"knee"`.

This branch avoids that entirely by never using a `"knee"` key at all — see below.

## ⚠️ Important: port labeling

**For ankle-only mode to work correctly, the ankle actuator's script must never contain a `"knee"` dictionary key.** As long as the actuator dictionary in the run script only defines `"ankle"`, the labeling is correct regardless of which `/dev/ttyACMx` port the actuator lands on:

```python
actuators = {
    "ankle": DephyActuator(tag="ankle", port="/dev/ttyACM0", ...),
}
```

There is no `"knee"` entry competing for that port, so there's nothing to mislabel.

**If you plug in additional USB-serial devices** (a second actuator for bench testing, a debug adapter, etc.), the ankle actuator's assigned port (`ttyACM0` vs `ttyACM1`) can shift depending on enumeration order. Always verify the correct port before running:

```bash
ls /dev/ttyACM*
```

For a setup that's robust regardless of plug-in order, consider adding a udev rule that pins a fixed device name to the actuator's USB serial number (not included in this branch, but recommended if you're running other USB-serial devices on the same Pi).

## What was added

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `examples/basic_motion_ankle_only.py` | Ankle-only version of `basic_motion.py`. Moves the ankle actuator through a test range of motion — no knee code included, for verifying a single-actuator setup is functional. |
